 
    {{ 'base.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag: preload: true }}
    {%- comment -%}
      The carousel: engine layout and the chrome the `carousel-*` snippets
      render. It replaces `swiper.css`, so this is one request where there was
      one before, for a fraction of the bytes.

      Loaded directly after `base.css` and written into the same `@layer
      overrides`, which is what lets a running track beat the section grid class
      it also carries — `.product-grid`, `.article-grid` and the rest are
      `display: grid` in `@layer components`, and a layer beats specificity. See
      the head of the file.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {{ 'carousel.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag: preload: true }}
    {%- comment -%}
      Loaded after base.css, and deliberately *not* preloaded.

      `preload: true` adds a `<link rel="preload">` on top of the stylesheet
      link, and Shopify also advertises it in Early Hints. The browser then
      counts one of those as unused and logs:

        "preloaded using link preload in Early Hints but not used within a few
         seconds from the window's load event"

      A plain stylesheet link is already render-blocking and already fetched at
      the highest priority, so the preload bought nothing and cost a console
      warning on every page load.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {{ 'header.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag: preload: true }}
    {{ 'product-card.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
    {%- comment -%}
      The search overlay and the shared empty state. Not preloaded: the overlay
      is behind a click on every page except /search, and the empty state is
      below the fold when it appears at all.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {{ 'search.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
    {%- comment -%}
      The footer and its composition blocks. Below the fold on every page, so it
      is linked rather than preloaded: the browser fetches it at normal priority
      while the part of the page the customer is actually looking at renders.
    {%- endcomment -%}
